  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong surface high pressure system centered over CNY will\ncontinue to bring pleasant weather with seasonable temperatures\nthru Sunday. Highs in the upper 70s to low 80s today. Clear \nskies and light winds tonight will allow for temps to fall into \nthe low to mid 50s. A few of our colder spots could see upper \n40s for lows. Some warmer air is expected to round the top of\nthe ridge centered over the central US and move into the area \non Saturday and Sunday. Highs are forecast to climb into the low\nto mid 80s. With dewpoints in the 50s, it will still feel \nsomewhat comfortable outside. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOn Sunday, the strong ridge over the central portion of the\ncountry should have a weakening in the NW quadrant, allowing a \nshortwave trough to dig into the Great Lakes region. This would\nallow for some light rain showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwest of I-81 in the afternoon. The ridge that is currently over \nus will slide to the NE and should continue to have enough \ninfluence to squash a lot of precipitation development, \nespecially east of I-81. We are seeing signals for more showers\nand storms starting Monday night into Wednesday as a stronger\nshortwave moves into the region from the north central US. This\ntrough will strengthen and develop a surface low somewhere\nbetween the eastern Ohio River Valley and southern Ontario Mon \nNight/Tues AM. This surface low will deepen as it tracks east.\nScattered showers with some isolated thunder will be possible, \nthru Wednesday. A low track to the south should keep severe\nweather chances to our west and south, while a low that develops\nover southern Ontario and moves into NY could allow for some\nbetter severe storm dynamics to develop over portions of the\nCWA. The current guidance is too varied to have strong\nconfidence in either solution so we will continue to monitor how\nthe pattern develops.\n\nThe trough axis should be east of us by Thursday, bringing NNW\nflow and pushing high pressure back into the area. Temps will be\nseasonable to slightly cooler than normal with dry conditions\nheading into the weekend.",
